From 2f64a1b0ee2b35cd858a8b601b4d425216e73ae5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pseudo <cdodge@pseudoseed.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 06:43:08 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 34/35] [Spec 250][Phase: review] fix: the approval path had no
 answer for a dead network, and the proxy had no bound on the way back
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two blocking findings from the codex lane's review of PR #266. Both real, both
on the approval surface.

## 1. Pre-submit transport failures escaped as rejected promises

`send` did a bare `await fetchImpl(...)` and FOUR of its five call sites had no
catch: opening the human session, issuing the capability, minting the nonce, and
the synchronous fallback. Only the async submit was guarded. `GateApproval` had a
`finally` and no `catch`, so a proxy disconnect during any of those four stopped
the spinner and said NOTHING — no error, no unconfirmed state, no outcome at all.

This is the defect class the last 11 phases were spent killing, on the
highest-stakes surface in the product: the human believes they approved, the
builder never advances, and nothing says which happened. It survived every round
because reviewers read the outcome vocabulary, which is careful, and nobody asked
what happens when `fetch` itself rejects. A rich taxonomy of answers is not the
same as answering.

The fix is a type rather than a `try`. `send` returns `Sent` —
`({reached: true} & Json) | {reached: false, error}` — so a transport failure is a
value. `reached: false` is not assignable to anything that reads `.status`, so
the compiler asks the question at all five call sites and a sixth cannot inherit
the bug by forgetting a `try`.

The outcomes are deliberately NOT the same:

- BEFORE a submit, nothing was ever asked for, so the gate provably did not move.
  `AGENT_UNREACHABLE_HUMAN_SESSION`, `AGENT_UNREACHABLE_CAPABILITY`,
  `AGENT_UNREACHABLE_NONCE` — definite, none of them `unconfirmed`. The session
  step also warns its single-use token may have been spent even though the reply
  was lost.
- ON a submit, the request may have arrived and only the reply been lost.
  `GATE_APPROVAL_UNCONFIRMED`, for both the async route and the synchronous
  fallback, which approves before it answers.

Flattening those into one "network error" would have been the same defect in a
tidier coat: telling someone to check a gate that cannot have changed teaches
them `unconfirmed` is ordinary noise, which is how the rare real one gets ignored.

`GateApproval` gains a catch as a backstop for defects rather than for the
network, reporting `unconfirmed` — an unexpected throw says nothing about whether
the agent acted.

Six tests, one per outcome. Restoring the throw: 6 failed, 19 passed, and they
fail BY REJECTION, which is the bug itself.

## 2. The upstream response was buffered with no limit

Every chunk pushed into an array with nothing watching the total. Same defect as
the request-body bound fixed in 24aeeebb3, on the return path, and the worse half:
a request body comes from an authenticated caller, a response body comes from
whatever the configured target is, so an operator misconfiguration or an agent
that streams without end made this server buffer without end.

`MAX_PROXIED_RESPONSE_BYTES` is 1 MiB and a separate number from the request
bound, because they describe different traffic. Exceeding it settles a new
`oversized` outcome and aborts the upstream — not merely stops reading, which
leaves the target streaming into a connection nobody drains.

`oversized` is its own kind, not `unreachable` and not a truncated `answered`.
Forwarding the first megabyte as if it were the whole reply is a partial answer
reading as a complete one, on the route that decides whether a gate was approved.

THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS FIX HAD THE BUG IT WAS FIXING. `destroy()` makes the
stream emit `error` synchronously and that handler settles `unreachable`, so
settling after the teardown reported a host that was answering as one that could
not be reached — the truthful outcome losing a race to a vaguer one. `settle` is
once-only, so claiming the outcome BEFORE tearing down is what makes it safe.
The test caught it.

The test's upstream sends past the bound and never ends the response, so a proxy
that only stopped reading would hang. Removing the bound: it fails by timing out
at 120s, which is what an unbounded buffer does.

Verified: agentProxy 30 passed, approval 25 passed, fork web suite 2990 passed,
contracts + server + web typecheck clean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
